# Copy this file to `.env` on the server, next to docker-compose.prod.yml.
# docker compose auto-loads `.env` from the same directory.
#
# THIS FILE IS NEVER SHIPPED TO A SERVER BY ANYTHING (HD-199). A deploy replaces the
# repository-owned config listed in ops/deploy/synced-paths.txt and deliberately does not
# touch `.env`: it holds every secret, and it holds the decisions that must survive a
# deploy — the image tag and the memory ceiling below. That boundary is what makes the
# rollback story a single one: what you set here still applies after the next deploy,
# without anybody remembering anything.
#
# EVERY REQUIRED VALUE HERE SHIPS EMPTY, AND THAT IS THE POINT (HD-200). The guards that
# protect an installation — ${VAR:?...} in the compose files, the length check on
# JWT_SECRET in the application — all fail on ABSENCE, and a placeholder is the one thing
# that is not absent. A template that filled them in produces an installation that starts,
# works, and is wrong: DB_PASSWORD=DB_PASSWORD does not fail, it AGREES with the Postgres
# container it also seeds, and the instance comes up on a database whose password is
# printed in a public repository. So an unedited copy of this file CANNOT start the stack,
# by design. Each refusal names one variable; fill that one in and the next names itself.
# Sealed by src/test/java/com/hamstrack/ops/EnvTemplateGuardTest.java, which reads the
# guarded set out of the compose files and application.properties rather than out of a
# list somebody has to remember to update.
#
# A DOLLAR SIGN IN ANY VALUE HERE IS NOT A CHARACTER — IT IS AN INTERPOLATION.
# docker compose expands `$NAME` and `${NAME}` inside `.env` values, and an undefined name
# expands to NOTHING. So a generated password of the form `Vk5$mT8pQr2zXn6w` reaches the
# container as `Vk5` — three characters, silently, with no warning from anything. This
# applies to EVERY value in this file, not to any one variable: DB_PASSWORD, MAIL_PASSWORD,
# SEED_ADMIN_PASSWORD, the Grafana admin password in the observability stack, and anything
# added later. Two ways out, and pick one before you paste:
#   * escape it by doubling — `$$` produces one literal `$`; or
#   * generate a value that has none. `openssl rand -base64 48` never emits `$` (its
#     alphabet is A-Za-z0-9+/=), so anything produced by the command this file recommends
#     is safe; anything a human or another generator chose is not, and that is the whole
#     of the difference.
# The failure is worst where nothing can detect it: a truncated SEED_ADMIN_PASSWORD seeds
# an administrator with a password the operator never chose and cannot see.

# ── Container image ────────────────────────────────────────────────
# GitHub owner/org that hosts the ghcr.io/<owner>/hamstrack image.
# MUST be lowercase (GitHub image paths are case-sensitive). Running the published
# image? That owner is `zherikhov`.
#
# Empty because ${GITHUB_OWNER:?...} in docker-compose.prod.yml only fires on absence: a
# placeholder owner interpolates cleanly into an image path and moves the failure to pull
# time, as a 404 on a repository nobody named.
GITHUB_OWNER=

# Which tag of that image to run. Empty (or absent) = `latest`.
#
# THIS IS THE ROLLBACK LEVER, and it is here rather than in docker-compose.prod.yml on
# purpose: a pin written into a synced compose file is wiped by the next deploy, silently
# and at the worst possible moment. Set APP_IMAGE_TAG=0.17.0 and `docker compose -f
# docker-compose.prod.yml -f docker-compose.observability.yml up -d`; it survives every
# subsequent deploy by construction.
#
# What the automated deploy does with it: the applier compares this value with
# .deployed-image-tag (the tag the last apply placed configuration beside) and REFUSES
# while the two disagree, so the deploy that follows a fresh pin goes red on purpose —
# nothing pins the configuration beside the image, and a config change synced onto a
# rolled-back image can re-apply what the rollback was undoing (docs/release-checklist.md).
# A pin that has held still is a steady state: the deploy proceeds and re-pulls the pinned
# tag. A hand run may override two ways, and they are not the same thing: --allow-pinned
# applies THIS RUN and leaves .deployed-image-tag alone, so the next unattended deploy
# refuses again (use it for a config fix needed during a rollback); --adopt-pin applies AND
# re-stamps, which is how you say this tag is now the intended version. Set it HERE, in
# this file, never as an exported shell variable: Compose gives the process environment
# precedence over .env, so an exported pin would deploy a tag no later run can see, and the
# applier refuses it for exactly that reason.
#
# Un-pinning is not a memory task either: while this names anything other than `latest`,
# the drift check publishes hamstrack_deploy_image_pinned and the DeployImagePinned alert
# says so after six hours — either it was forgotten, or that version is now the intended
# one, and both deserve a sentence to a person.
#
# Self-hosting? Pin a release LINE (`0.4`) or an exact version (`0.4.3`) here rather than
# tracking `latest`, which moves on every main build. See docs/self-hosting.md#upgrading.
#
# Empty is harmless in the way APP_MEMORY_LIMIT= is harmless: Compose reads it, not
# Spring, so it falls back to `latest` instead of stopping the boot. It ships empty rather
# than with a placeholder because no value in this template may satisfy its own guard.
APP_IMAGE_TAG=

# ── Resources ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Memory ceiling for the app CONTAINER (compose `mem_limit`; any docker size
# suffix — 1g / 1536m). Default 1g. This is the heap dial: the image runs the JVM
# with -XX:MaxRAMPercentage=50, so the heap is half of whatever you set here —
# 1g → 512 MB heap, 2g → 1 GB heap — and the other half covers the non-heap memory
# a JVM in a container still needs (metaspace, thread stacks, code cache, direct
# buffers). Sizing it too close to the heap gets the container OOM-KILLED (exit
# 137) instead of throwing OutOfMemoryError, which is the harder failure to
# diagnose. Do NOT set it to the size of the whole box: PostgreSQL
# (POSTGRES_MEMORY_LIMIT, default 512m), Caddy (CADDY_MEMORY_LIMIT, default 128m)
# and — if you run it — the observability stack (whose own mem_limits sum to ~1 GB)
# need their share. Those are ceilings and not reservations, so the four of them
# legitimately declare more than a small host has; what they must not do is all be
# in use at once.
#
# UPGRADING FROM BEFORE 0.17.0 ON A HOST LARGER THAN 2 GB: leaving this at the
# default is a heap CUT. The JVM used to take ~25% of HOST RAM, so a 4 GB host ran
# a ~1 GB heap and an 8 GB host ~2 GB, where 1g here gives 512 MB on both.
# Break-even is a 2 GB host; below it you gain. Full note, with the sizing rule:
# "The heap is bounded from 0.17.0" in docs/self-hosting.md.
#
# 512 MB is also the heap REPORTS_MAX_ROWS (further down) is costed against — its
# worst case of ~95 MB per request is 95 MB *of this* — so raising either one is
# the occasion to re-read the other.
#
# From 4g up the 50/50 split over-reserves, because the non-heap need is largely
# CONSTANT: metaspace and the code cache do not grow with the heap. Pair a bigger
# limit with an explicit heap — JAVA_TOOL_OPTIONS below, roughly this limit minus
# ~700 MB. (At 2g the waste is only ~300 MB, so a 4 GB host needs nothing beyond
# the limit itself.)
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# -Xmx IS THE ONLY FORM THAT WORKS. JAVA_TOOL_OPTIONS=-XX:MaxRAMPercentage=75
# loses to the image's own copy of that flag and changes NOTHING — and the JVM
# prints "Picked up JAVA_TOOL_OPTIONS: …" either way. That line says the variable
# was READ, not that it was applied, so the percentage form looks like it worked.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# Unlike the application settings below, an EMPTY value here is harmless: Compose
# reads it, not Spring, so "APP_MEMORY_LIMIT=" falls back to 1g rather than
# stopping the boot.
#APP_MEMORY_LIMIT=1g
# How long the app gets between SIGTERM and SIGKILL, in seconds. READ TWICE: Docker
# Compose puts it in `stop_grace_period` on the app service, and the app itself binds
# it as app.mail.async.stop-grace-seconds. One variable on purpose — the app has to
# know its own grace, because it waits MAIL_ASYNC_SHUTDOWN_DRAIN_SECONDS for queued
# mail and then writes whatever is left to failed_email, and it REFUSES TO START if
# those two steps would not fit inside this number.
# DOCKER'S OWN DEFAULT IS 10s, which is shorter than the 15s mail drain, so a compose
# file without a stop_grace_period line kills the JVM mid-drain and the queued
# account-critical mail is lost with no row and no log line — that is why
# docker-compose.prod.yml states it, and why the Quick-start file in docs/self-hosting.md
# states it too. RAISE THIS FIRST if you want a longer drain:
# raising MAIL_ASYNC_SHUTDOWN_DRAIN_SECONDS alone is refused at boot. Valid range 1-600.
# Empty is NOT harmless here, unlike APP_MEMORY_LIMIT: Compose falls back to 30 while
# the app aborts on the empty value. Leave the line commented out to take the default.
# That is a fact about how THIS file reaches the app — docker-compose.prod.yml passes it
# through `env_file: .env`, so an empty string arrives as an empty string. A compose file
# that instead names it under `environment:` as ${APP_STOP_GRACE_SECONDS:-30} substitutes
# the default for a blank as well as for an absent value, and boots. The habit is the same
# either way: comment the line out rather than blanking it, because only one of the two
# arrangements tells you that you did something.
#APP_STOP_GRACE_SECONDS=30
# Explicit heap, for a container limit of 4g or more (see just above). Passed to
# the JVM, not to Compose. Leave it unset below 4g — the image's 50% of the
# container limit is the right split there.
#JAVA_TOOL_OPTIONS=-Xmx3g

# WHAT THE APP ACTUALLY GOT, rather than what these lines intend: from 0.18.0 the
# application logs ONE line at startup naming its resolved maximum heap, whether that
# came from an explicit -Xmx or was derived from a percentage, the garbage collector,
# the container limit it can see, and REPORTS_MAX_ROWS beside it (HD-179):
#
#   docker compose logs app | grep "Memory: max heap"
#   Memory: max heap 512 MB = 536870912 bytes (MaxHeapSize; derived from
#   -XX:MaxRAMPercentage=50, no -Xmx); GC SerialGC; container memory limit 1024 MB;
#   app.reports.max-rows=20000
#
# Read it after any change here. It is the only artefact in this deployment that
# reports a measurement instead of an intention, and it is what settles the
# JAVA_TOOL_OPTIONS trap above: "Picked up JAVA_TOOL_OPTIONS: …" says a variable was
# READ, this line says what was APPLIED. The byte figure is HotSpot's MaxHeapSize, so
# it can be compared directly with `java -XX:+PrintFlagsFinal -version`; if that word
# reads "Runtime.maxMemory" instead, the JVM would not state MaxHeapSize and the number
# is usable heap, which can sit a little below it.
#
# AND THE COLLECTOR IS PART OF THE SIZING, WHICH IS WHY IT IS ON THAT LINE. At
# APP_MEMORY_LIMIT=1g the JVM is below its "server-class machine" threshold and
# ergonomically selects SerialGC — single-threaded, stop-the-world; at 2g, same image
# and same flags, it selects G1 (both measured 2026-09-01 on eclipse-temurin:21-jre-alpine,
# the tag the app image is built from, with 2 CPUs). That
# is the difference between a 50 ms pause and a multi-second one, and the likeliest
# explanation of the 4.99 s pause measured on a 1g container during the 2026-08-31 load
# window (the collector itself was not recorded that day). If pause times are the symptom,
# the ceiling above is the dial that changes the collector.

# ── Memory ceilings for the other two containers (HD-180) ──────────────
# Same units and the same nature as APP_MEMORY_LIMIT: read by Docker Compose
# (`mem_limit`), never by the app, so docker size suffixes (512m / 2g) and an EMPTY
# value falls back to the default instead of stopping the boot.
#
# These exist because until 0.18.0 postgres and caddy carried NO limit while every
# observability container did — so under host memory pressure the kernel picked the
# victim, and the app (the only bounded process) was as likely to be it as the one
# that grew. A limit is CONTAINMENT: the offender is killed and restarted in its own
# cgroup. It is NOT a promise the box cannot run out of memory — the defaults below
# plus 1g of app plus ~1g of observability declare 2688 MiB of ceilings on the 1909
# MiB production host, which is stated plainly at the top of docker-compose.prod.yml
# rather than hidden in a sum.
#
# 512m for PostgreSQL is ~2× its measured peak RSS (~240 MB) at the settings below.
# RAISE IT WHENEVER YOU RAISE POSTGRES_SHARED_BUFFERS, POSTGRES_WORK_MEM OR
# DB_POOL_MAX_SIZE, and never set it below what the server is configured to use: a
# cgroup ceiling under the dials turns a tuning value into an OOM kill of a backend — or
# of the postmaster, which takes every session with it. Those three are on the list
# because they are the terms in what this ceiling has to contain: shared_buffers is a
# floor under it, and work_mem × sort nodes × backends is the product on top of it.
# THE ARITHMETIC, SPELLED THE SAME WAY WHEREVER IT APPEARS (it lives in
# docker-compose.prod.yml, beside the dial): 4MB × ~4 nodes × ~12 backends
# (DB_POOL_MAX_SIZE 10, plus the postgres-exporter and a psql session) ≈ 190 MB. Take
# the pool 10 → 50 and it is 4MB × 4 × 52 ≈ 830 MB against 512m.
#POSTGRES_MEMORY_LIMIT=512m
# 128m for Caddy is ~5× its measured peak (~24 MB), deliberately looser than
# PostgreSQL's ~2×: it is the only container on 80/443, so an OOM kill here is a
# site-wide outage, and unused ceiling costs nothing.
#CADDY_MEMORY_LIMIT=128m

# ── PostgreSQL memory dials (HD-225) ───────────────────────────────────
# Passed to the server as `postgres -c name=value` by docker-compose.prod.yml. They
# take PostgreSQL's units (128MB, 1GB, 4MB). A value the server cannot parse makes it
# REFUSE TO START while `docker compose up -d` still exits 0 — change one at a time
# and check `docker compose ps`.
#
# EFFECTIVE_CACHE_SIZE IS NOT AN ALLOCATION. It is what the query planner BELIEVES is
# cached — shared_buffers plus the OS page cache it can expect to reach — so it costs
# no memory and changes which plans look cheap. The image default is 4GB, which on
# Hamstrack's own 1909 MiB production host claimed more than twice the machine's RAM;
# 512MB is shared_buffers (128 MB) plus the low end of the page cache measured there
# under load (387 MB). SET IT FROM YOUR OWN HOST, IN BOTH DIRECTIONS: roughly
# shared_buffers + the page cache this database can really expect. On a dedicated
# database host the usual starting point is ~75% of RAM — a box that also runs the app,
# Caddy and the observability stack is not one. AND UNDER ~1 GB OF RAM, LOWER IT:
# 512MB on a 512 MB VPS claims the whole machine as cache and is the same mistake as the
# image's 4GB, one order of magnitude down. Work out shared_buffers + what the page cache
# actually holds there (`free -m`) and use that; 192MB is the starting point that goes
# with the 64MB shared_buffers below. ~1 GB is the same threshold all three dials here
# use — between 1 and 2 GB the defaults as shipped are already the right answer.
#POSTGRES_EFFECTIVE_CACHE_SIZE=512MB
# Shared buffers ARE an allocation, and are inside POSTGRES_MEMORY_LIMIT above. The
# stock advice of 25% of RAM assumes the database owns the box; on a shared host the
# page cache does much of the same work and this memory is not free — it comes out of
# the JVM's. Raise this, effective_cache_size and POSTGRES_MEMORY_LIMIT together when you
# give the box more RAM — and under ~1 GB of RAM go the other way, to 64MB, where the
# allocation is a real subtraction from the JVM's heap. (Same ~1 GB threshold as the row
# above and the one below: one number for all three dials, not three.)
#POSTGRES_SHARED_BUFFERS=128MB
# work_mem is PER SORT OR HASH NODE, not per connection: one query with three sorts
# and a hash join can take four times this, and the app opens up to DB_POOL_MAX_SIZE
# (default 10) connections. The honest worst case is work_mem × nodes × backends —
# 4MB × ~4 × ~12 ≈ 190 MB, the derivation quoted everywhere else. THIS DIAL IS ITSELF A
# REASON TO RAISE POSTGRES_MEMORY_LIMIT, alongside POSTGRES_SHARED_BUFFERS and
# DB_POOL_MAX_SIZE: it is a factor in the same product, and doubling it doubles the
# ceiling that has to contain it. Lower spills sorts to temp files (slow); higher swaps
# the host (slower, and it takes the JVM's GC with it), so under ~1 GB of RAM go DOWN,
# to 2MB. AND ON A ROOMY HOST RAISE POSTGRES_SHM_SIZE WITH IT: a parallel worker's share
# of a sort is allocated in /dev/shm, which Docker sizes at 64 MB, and exhausting it
# fails with `could not resize shared memory segment … No space left on device` — a
# message that names neither this variable nor the memory limit.
#POSTGRES_WORK_MEM=4MB
# /dev/shm for the postgres container. The default IS Docker's own 64m, so setting it to
# 64m changes nothing; it exists so the dial the block above sends you to is in this file
# rather than in a compose file an upgrade replaces.
#POSTGRES_SHM_SIZE=64m

# ── Deployment model ───────────────────────────────────────────────
# cloud = hosted SaaS (S3 storage by default); dc = self-hosted (local
# disk by default). Storage/other per-model defaults come from this.
#
# SELF-HOSTING? SET THIS TO `dc`, AND SET IT — do not empty it. This file is
# written for Hamstrack's own hosted install, so it ships `cloud`, and running an
# on-premise box on the cloud profile is two wrong defaults at once: PUBLIC_SIGNUP_
# ENABLED defaults to TRUE (anyone who reaches the URL can create an account, where
# `dc` defaults it to false), and attachment storage defaults to S3 — with no bucket
# configured, so uploads fail at runtime rather than at startup. Neither is guarded,
# because a profile name is a valid choice and not a missing value.
#
# Emptying the line is NOT the fix and lands you back on cloud: docker-compose.prod.yml
# reads it as ${SPRING_PROFILES_ACTIVE:-cloud}, and `:-` treats empty exactly as unset.
SPRING_PROFILES_ACTIVE=cloud

# ── Database ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# These seed BOTH sides — the bundled Postgres container's POSTGRES_USER/POSTGRES_PASSWORD
# and the application's datasource — which is exactly why neither may carry a placeholder:
# a placeholder does not disagree with anything, it agrees with itself, and the stack comes
# up on a production database whose credentials are published. Both are guarded by
# ${...:?...} in docker-compose.prod.yml, so an empty value refuses the `up` and names
# which one is missing. Generate the password rather than inventing one:
#   openssl rand -base64 24
DB_USERNAME=
DB_PASSWORD=
# Connection-pool sizing (optional). Defaults: max 10, min-idle 5. Raise the max
# for higher concurrency, but keep (max × app replicas) under Postgres
# max_connections. With open-in-view=false connections are held only per
# transaction, so a small pool goes a long way.
#
# THE POOL IS ALSO A MEMORY DIAL ON THE DATABASE, AND max_connections IS NOT THE
# BOUND THAT BITES FIRST. POSTGRES_WORK_MEM (above) is charged per sort or hash node
# per BACKEND, so the worst case is work_mem × nodes × backends and this variable is
# the `backends`: at the defaults 4MB × ~4 nodes × ~12 backends (this pool, plus the
# postgres-exporter and a psql session) is ~190 MB against a 512m POSTGRES_MEMORY_LIMIT,
# and taking the pool 10 → 50 makes it 4MB × 4 × 52 ≈ 830 MB — comfortably under a stock
# max_connections of 100, and comfortably over the cgroup ceiling, where the outcome
# is an OOM-killed backend or postmaster rather than a refused connection. Raise
# POSTGRES_MEMORY_LIMIT (or lower POSTGRES_WORK_MEM) in the same edit.
#DB_POOL_MAX_SIZE=10
#DB_POOL_MIN_IDLE=5
# How long a transaction may wait for a row lock before giving up, in milliseconds.
# FROM 0.17.0 THIS APPLIES TO EVERY TRANSACTION, not only the few that lock on
# purpose: an ordinary edit queued behind a long-running change now fails after 3s
# with a retryable 409 instead of waiting indefinitely. It is issued together with
# DB_STATEMENT_TIMEOUT_MS below, because statement_timeout counts lock-wait time —
# without this, a contended write would be cancelled by THAT bound and answered a
# 422 telling the caller not to retry, when retrying is exactly what works.
# Applied with SET LOCAL, never on the datasource, so Flyway migrations are
# unaffected and still wait as long as they need. Exceeding it is a 409 +
# Retry-After. Valid range 100–60000 — an out-of-range value (0 means
# "wait for ever" to PostgreSQL) fails startup rather than being clamped silently.
# To turn it off, leave the line COMMENTED OUT (as below) — not blank. "DB_LOCK_
# TIMEOUT_MS=" is an empty value, not an absent one, and binding empty to the
# app's primitive int aborts startup; only an absent variable falls back to 3000.
# CAPPED IN PRACTICE BY THE LINE BELOW: DB_STATEMENT_TIMEOUT_MS must stay at least
# 2x this value, so while that one is at its default 10000, this may not exceed
# 5000 — a higher value here stops the boot naming BOTH properties. Raise them
# together.
#DB_LOCK_TIMEOUT_MS=3000
# How long ANY ONE STATEMENT may run before PostgreSQL cancels it, in milliseconds.
# Applied with SET LOCAL to every transaction the app opens (not to Flyway, which
# runs its own — a migration is allowed to take minutes where a report is not).
# Exceeding it is a 422 with errorType STATEMENT_BUDGET_EXCEEDED and no Retry-After.
# NEW IN 0.17.0, AND IT CAN TURN A SLOW REPORT INTO AN ERROR ON A LARGE INSTALL:
# before this release a query could run for ever. If a report, a search or a member
# removal starts failing after upgrading, raise this. See docs/self-hosting.md
# "Statements are bounded from 0.17.0" for a size-to-value table.
# Must be at least 2x DB_LOCK_TIMEOUT_MS or the app refuses to start (the smaller
# bound always fires first, and a statement bound below the lock bound would replace
# the retryable 409 with a 422 that is not retryable). Valid range 1000-600000; 0
# means "no bound" to PostgreSQL and is refused. THE 2x RULE IS THE BINDING ONE:
# with DB_LOCK_TIMEOUT_MS at its default 3000, the smallest value that boots is
# 6000, not the 1000 the range suggests.
# RAISING THIS IS NOT FREE: every second you add is a second one request may hold
# one of your DB_POOL_MAX_SIZE connections (default 10, set further up this file), so the
# same pool serves fewer concurrent slow requests. Past ~30s, raise the pool too.
# Same "comment it out, never blank" rule as DB_LOCK_TIMEOUT_MS —
# "DB_STATEMENT_TIMEOUT_MS=" is an empty value, not an absent one, and binding
# empty to the app's primitive int aborts startup.
#DB_STATEMENT_TIMEOUT_MS=10000
# Whether PostgreSQL's DETAIL / HINT / POSITION lines reach the application log.
# OFF by default, and that default is a privacy floor rather than a tuning choice:
# the driver folds DETAIL into the exception message and Hibernate logs it before
# any application code can redact it, so on a duplicate-key error the log would
# carry the colliding VALUES — on the invitation path, a third party's full email
# address, from a request they never made.
# WHAT OFF COSTS YOU, AND IT IS NOT ONLY THE ONE YOU LEAK: every server error loses
# its detail, including FLYWAY'S — a migration that fails during an upgrade names
# the index or constraint and NOT the rows that collided, and reports no line
# position on a syntax error. That is the case worth knowing about in advance,
# because it is the one you read at 2am mid-deploy.
# TURN IT ON FOR ONE SESSION, THEN REMOVE THE LINE. Set it true, `docker compose up
# -d`, reproduce, read the log, put it back. Do NOT leave it on: your logs then hold
# your users' addresses and whatever else a failing statement was carrying, wherever
# those logs end up.
# UNLIKE the two timeouts above, a BLANK value here does not stop the boot — it is
# bound as a driver property, not as an int, and an empty string reads as false.
# So "DB_LOG_SERVER_ERROR_DETAIL=" is the same as off. Verified against a running
# app, because the convention above would have made you expect otherwise.
#DB_LOG_SERVER_ERROR_DETAIL=false

# ── Backups ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# NOT CONFIGURED HERE, AND ON PURPOSE. The scheduled database backup is a HOST
# systemd timer (hamstrack-backup.timer), not a container in this compose project,
# so it reads /etc/hamstrack/backup.env instead of this file. Three reasons:
# everything here is passed wholesale into the app container by `env_file:`, and
# backup settings have no business in the application's environment; the script
# needs none of the secrets above (it `docker exec`s into the postgres container
# and uses that container's own $POSTGRES_USER/$POSTGRES_DB); and systemd's
# EnvironmentFile parser is not Compose's, so one file would couple two parsers
# to one syntax.
#
# Template with every variable and its default:  ops/backup/backup.env.example
# How to install and run it:                     docs/self-hosting.md#backups
# Why it is built this way:                      docs/design/production-backups-proposal.md
#
# Not on AWS? `BACKUP_S3_ENDPOINT` points the upload at MinIO / Backblaze B2 /
# Wasabi, and `BACKUP_TARGET=local` drops the object store entirely.

# ── Security ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# JWT_SECRET signs every access token. Min 32 bytes or the app fails fast at startup,
# and the refusal carries the generation command so you need not come back here for it.
# Generate with: openssl rand -base64 48
#
# This line used to read a REPLACE_WITH_... placeholder that was 34 bytes long — i.e. long
# enough to PASS the length check. An operator who copied this file and edited only what
# they recognised got an instance that started, worked, and signed every token with a
# string published on GitHub; anyone able to read the repository could mint a token for it,
# including one claiming to be an administrator. The application now refuses that exact
# value, and the other one this project has published, on top of refusing anything short.
# If an upgrade suddenly stops booting because the secret is "a value published in
# Hamstrack's own documentation", that is this — and it was already true before the upgrade
# said so out loud.
#
# WHAT REPLACING THE SECRET DOES, EXACTLY: every access token signed with the old key is
# rejected immediately, and NOTHING ELSE. It does not end sessions — refresh tokens are
# random values independent of this secret, so each client takes one 401 and silently
# re-issues from its refresh cookie. If you think the published value was used against this
# instance, rotation is the first step and not the last: also revoke every refresh session
# (DELETE FROM refresh_tokens), delete every unused invite/reset link
# (DELETE FROM password_resets WHERE used_at IS NULL — an admin session forged with the old
# key can issue one, and that row outlives both the rotation and the DELETE before it), and
# audit your admin accounts.
JWT_SECRET=
# Access-token lifetime (ISO-8601 duration). Short by design — the refresh
# cookie renews it; a longer value widens the replay window for a leaked token.
#JWT_ACCESS_TOKEN_TTL=PT30M
# Auth rate-limiter: trust the rightmost X-Forwarded-For for per-IP keying.
# The bundled docker-compose.prod.yml DEFAULTS this to true (traffic always comes
# through Caddy, which strips client-supplied XFF) and reads the value from here
# when you set one — `${RATE_LIMIT_TRUST_FORWARDED_FOR:-true}`, so this line is a
# real lever and not advice the compose file overrides. Set it false when the app
# port is directly reachable (you published 8080 to front it with your own proxy,
# for instance): with it true, any client can spoof X-Forwarded-For and be keyed as
# somebody else, which makes the per-IP auth limit unbounded. Leave it alone if you
# run the bundled Caddy.
#RATE_LIMIT_TRUST_FORWARDED_FOR=true

# ── Uploads (optional) ─────────────────────────────────────────────
# Per-file business limit (enforced in-app) and the hard servlet ceiling.
# MAX_FILE_SIZE must stay <= MAX_UPLOAD_SIZE; match your proxy body limit to
# the ceiling. ALLOWED_EXTENSIONS is a case-insensitive comma-separated list.
#ATTACHMENT_MAX_FILE_SIZE=20MB
#ATTACHMENT_MAX_UPLOAD_SIZE=25MB
#ATTACHMENT_ALLOWED_EXTENSIONS=png,jpg,jpeg,gif,webp,bmp,svg,pdf,txt,csv,log,md,json,doc,docx,xls,xlsx,ppt,pptx,zip

# ── Board ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Max issues a single board query returns — a guard against unbounded board
# loads on very large projects. Raise only if boards legitimately need more.
# Valid range 1–20000 (20000 is the most assembled issues ONE unpaged response may
# build, the same budget the agile planning view uses) — an out-of-range value fails
# startup rather than being clamped silently. A 0 here used to bind quietly and empty
# every board in the install.
#BOARD_MAX_ISSUES=500

# ── Classification (labels, components, versions) ──────────────────
# Max labels attachable to one issue. A DoS guard, identical in DC and Cloud;
# exceeding it is a 422, not a silent truncation. Valid range 1–100 — an
# out-of-range value fails startup rather than being clamped silently.
#MAX_LABELS_PER_ISSUE=20
# Max labels in one workspace's catalog (any member may create labels, so the
# catalog needs a ceiling). 422 when full. Valid range 1–100000.
#MAX_LABELS_PER_WORKSPACE=1000
# Max components in one project's catalog. Creating them needs a project curator,
# but that is no volume barrier (anyone can create their own workspace and owns
# every project in it), and HQL name resolution loads every visible project's whole
# component catalog on each search. 422 when full. Valid range 1–100000.
#MAX_COMPONENTS_PER_PROJECT=500
# Max versions linkable to ONE issue, PER LINK TYPE — i.e. this many fix versions
# and, independently, this many affects versions. 422 beyond it. Valid range 1–100.
#MAX_VERSION_LINKS_PER_ISSUE=20
# Max versions in one project's catalog — same reasoning as MAX_COMPONENTS_PER_PROJECT
# (curator-gated is not a volume barrier, and every visible project's version names are
# loaded on each search; the versions list endpoint is unpaged too). Archived and
# released versions count. 422 when full. Valid range 1–100000.
#MAX_VERSIONS_PER_PROJECT=500

# ── Agile (sprints, Scrum board, backlog ranking) ──────────────────
# Same posture as the classification caps: DoS guards and defaults, identical in DC
# and Cloud, no feature flag. All are validated — an out-of-range value fails startup
# rather than being clamped silently.
#
# Max issues rendered per section in the planning view (GET .../backlog). Together with
# AGILE_MAX_OPEN_SPRINTS this bounds the response size ((sprints + 1) x this). A
# truncated section still reports honest whole-section totals. Valid range 1–2000.
#AGILE_SECTION_MAX_ISSUES=300
# Max FUTURE + ACTIVE sprints in one project (COMPLETED ones are history and cannot be
# started, so they don't count). 422 on sprint creation when full. Valid range 1–100.
#AGILE_MAX_OPEN_SPRINTS=20
# NOTE — these two are validated TOGETHER as well: AGILE_SECTION_MAX_ISSUES ×
# (AGILE_MAX_OPEN_SPRINTS + 1) must not exceed 20000, because ONE GET .../backlog
# assembles that many issues in a single unpaged response. Both at their individual
# maxima would be ~202000 rows in one response — the app refuses to start instead of
# handing you an OOM at 3am.
#
# Default iteration length in days — the end date a sprint start assumes when the
# request carries none. Valid range 1–90.
#AGILE_DEFAULT_SPRINT_LENGTH_DAYS=14
# Max issue ids in one bulk "move to sprint" request; beyond it the request is a 400 and
# the client is expected to chunk. Valid range 1–500.
#AGILE_MAX_BULK_MOVE=100

# ── Reports ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Bounds on every report (created-vs-resolved flow today; cycle time, aging WIP,
# sprint burn-up and velocity as they land). Product bounds, NOT a plan or tier —
# identical in dc and cloud, no profile override. If reporting depth were ever
# limited per deployment it would be by lowering these numbers, never by a second
# code path. All three fail startup when out of range instead of being clamped.
#
# Widest window one report may span, in DAYS, counting both endpoints. A wider
# request is refused with 400 and this cap named in the message — it is never
# silently narrowed, because a chart of a window nobody asked for is exactly how a
# reporting feature earns "these numbers don't match what I expected". Valid range
# 1–3650; at daily buckets the window length IS the number of points in the
# response, which is what the upper bound guards.
# NOTE: a value UNDER 90 is fine and is not a trap — the endpoint's own default
# window is min(90, this), so the parameterless request the reports page makes on
# load is always inside the cap. What is never done is CLAMPING a window a caller
# explicitly asked for: that is a 400 naming this number.
#REPORTS_MAX_WINDOW_DAYS=365
# Most issue ROWS one report may materialise before it declares itself truncated
# (every report response carries meta.truncated + meta.cap, and the UI prints it).
# It does not bite on the flow report, which aggregates in PostgreSQL and returns
# one row per bucket; it is the budget for the row-level reports. Valid range
# 1–50000. The ceiling is a byte budget wearing a row count: a shipped row costs
# roughly 1.9 KB of transient heap at worst, so 50000 is ~95 MB for a single request
# — 95 MB OF the 512 MB heap that the default APP_MEMORY_LIMIT=1g gives you (see
# Resources at the top). Size this against the heap you actually run, not the default.
#REPORTS_MAX_ROWS=20000
# How many report requests ONE USER may make per minute across the whole reports
# surface (every .../projects/*/reports/** endpoint, not per report) PLUS
# POST .../workspaces/*/search/insights, the Insights panel — a report that does NOT
# live under the reports path (its dataset is an HQL query, so it hangs off /search)
# and is bound to this limiter explicitly rather than by prefix. For the
# project-scoped reports this is the only bound on the WORK a report does — the
# window cap above bounds the response array, not the cost of producing it, and the
# "open at window start" balance is O(project history) by design while
# Cache-Control: private means no shared cache ever absorbs a repeat. Insights is the
# one exception to "only bound": it is ALSO inside SEARCH_REQUESTS_PER_MINUTE below,
# so it spends both budgets and THE LOWER CONFIGURED VALUE BINDS — lowering EITHER
# property lowers the panel. Past the budget: 429 + Retry-After, never
# a narrowed or approximated report. Valid range 1–10000. THERE IS NO "UNLIMITED"
# VALUE: 0 is out of range and fails startup; the off switch is RATE_LIMIT_ENABLED,
# which turns off every limiter that HAS an off switch (not the backlog-rebalance
# cooldown, a fixed internal safety valve with no variable and no switch).
#REPORTS_REQUESTS_PER_MINUTE=60
# To keep any of the three REPORTS_* defaults above, leave the line COMMENTED OUT — not
# blank. "REPORTS_MAX_WINDOW_DAYS=" is an empty value, not an absent one, and binding
# empty to the app's primitive int aborts startup; only an absent variable falls back
# to the documented default. This matters more here than elsewhere: the window cap is
# quoted back to API callers inside a 400, so a value nobody chose would be presented
# to users as though somebody had.

# ── Search ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The HQL surface has its own per-principal budget, separate from the reports one
# above. Same shape as REPORTS_REQUESTS_PER_MINUTE: fails startup out of range,
# and an empty value aborts the boot rather than restoring the default.
#
# How many SEARCH-surface requests ONE USER may make per minute: POST .../search,
# GET .../search/schema, GET .../search/suggest, POST .../search/insights and EVERY
# SAVED-FILTER operation under .../filters/** (GET and DELETE included -- the binding
# is by path, not by method). Saved filters are on this budget because HQL validation
# is search-surface work wherever it is mounted: validating a filter builds the same
# resolution context .../search/schema pays for -- about eight statements, including a
# workspace-wide label projection and a full member scan -- so creating one with a
# deliberately invalid body was an unthrottled eight-query refusal loop. Charged here
# rather than to the reports pot because a saved filter IS a saved search. (The
# panel spends BOTH budgets, and the LOWER CONFIGURED VALUE binds — it sits on the
# reports limiter deliberately, because removing that binding would raise the panel's
# allowance to the search budget as a side effect, so lowering EITHER property lowers
# the panel). Search is not the cheap surface it looks like: up to 50 leaf predicates
# per query, a text ~ leaf is two unanchored unindexable LIKEs over a TEXT column,
# and the endpoint runs the whole predicate TWICE per request (count + page). Its own
# budget rather than the reports one because a person typing legitimately fires
# several requests a minute;
# 120 is roughly ten times ordinary SPA use. Past it: 429 + Retry-After. Valid range
# 1-10000. THERE IS NO "UNLIMITED" VALUE: 0 fails startup; the off switch is
# RATE_LIMIT_ENABLED. Leave the line COMMENTED OUT to keep the default — an empty
# "SEARCH_REQUESTS_PER_MINUTE=" aborts the boot rather than restoring 120.
#SEARCH_REQUESTS_PER_MINUTE=120

# ── The master switch ──────────────────────────────────────────────
# THE off switch, and the ONLY one: none of the individual limiter settings in this file
# accepts an "unlimited" value (0 is out of range on every one of them and fails startup),
# so setting this false is the only way to turn any of them off -- and it turns off ALL of
# them at once: the auth per-IP budget and login backoff, REPORTS_REQUESTS_PER_MINUTE,
# SEARCH_REQUESTS_PER_MINUTE and every INVITE_* ceiling below.
#
# "Every limiter that HAS an off switch" is the precise claim, not "every limiter". The
# backlog rank-rebalance cooldown is deliberately outside it: it is a fixed internal safety
# valve protecting the database from a whole-table rewrite storm, with no variable of its
# own and no way to disable it. A limiter that protects the database is not something an
# operator asks to remove.
#
# Turning it off does NOT stop mail_send_events rows being written or swept. Those rows are
# your forensic trail after a volume alert -- the metrics carry no addresses and no ids by
# design -- and a table that only grows while a switch is off would be the worse trade.
# LEAVE THIS ALONE IN PRODUCTION. It exists for load tests and for debugging a limiter you
# believe is misbehaving; every family above bounds an abuse vector that is reachable by
# anyone who can log in.
#
# LEAVE THE LINE COMMENTED OUT to keep the default. `RATE_LIMIT_ENABLED=` with nothing after
# it is an empty value, not an absent one, and it ABORTS THE BOOT: Spring converts a blank
# to null, the binder treats a converted null as bound rather than missing, so the
# ${RATE_LIMIT_ENABLED:true} default in application.properties never gets a turn and the
# primitive boolean refuses it. Measured, not assumed.
#
# Do NOT read that as a rule about booleans. RATE_LIMIT_TRUST_FORWARDED_FOR above is the same
# primitive type and is SAFE to leave empty -- because it reaches the container through
# docker-compose.prod.yml as ${RATE_LIMIT_TRUST_FORWARDED_FOR:-true}, and the shell's `:-`
# does restore a default on empty where Spring's `:` does not. The distinction is which
# default form the variable passes through, not what type it is.
#RATE_LIMIT_ENABLED=true

# ── Invitations ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Ceilings on the invitation MAILER, not on the invitations table. Without them any
# account that can log in can make this instance send mail to any address on the
# internet, without limit, by typing that address into the invite box of a workspace it
# created seconds ago -- and that mail carries YOUR domain's SPF/DKIM, so a stranger's
# spam complaints land on the same sending reputation that carries your verification and
# password-reset mail. The first symptom is usually that new users stop receiving their
# verification links: signup breaks silently, from a cause with no error in it.
#
# THREE WINDOWS AND NO PER-MINUTE ONE, on purpose. What is at risk is a MONTHLY quota
# (Resend's free tier is 3000 messages a month), and any per-minute ceiling loose enough
# for a human is about 28800 a day -- a rate window and a quota are different units. The
# hour bounds a burst, THE DAY IS THE QUOTA CONTROL, and the cooldown bounds harassment.
#
# All five fail startup out of range, and an EMPTY value aborts the boot rather than
# restoring the default. THERE IS NO "UNLIMITED" VALUE: 0 is out of range on every one of
# them; the off switch is RATE_LIMIT_ENABLED, and if you want no practical ceiling, write
# the top of the range. Identical in dc and cloud -- these are not mode switches.
#
# Invitations ONE ACCOUNT may send per hour and per day. Independent windows: spending the
# hourly allowance five times over ends the day, and the daily refusal does not lift when
# the hour rolls. THIS IS THE ONE YOU WILL RAISE: onboarding 300 people on install day
# hits the daily ceiling, and the fix is this line for a day, not a code change. Counted
# in memory per node, so N replicas allow up to N x these. Past either: 429 + Retry-After,
# and the message tells the admin their already-sent invitations are unaffected.
#INVITE_MAX_PER_SENDER_PER_HOUR=20
#INVITE_MAX_PER_SENDER_PER_DAY=100
# THE ONE THAT CLOSES THE ABUSE: minutes one account must wait before inviting THE SAME
# ADDRESS again, ACROSS EVERY WORKSPACE ON THE INSTANCE. The attack is one victim and a
# succession of workspaces the abuser creates for free, so any per-workspace bound looks
# at the wrong dimension. Its state lives in the mail_send_events table rather than in
# memory, and NOT for durability: derived from the invitations table it would be cleared
# by the victim's own "decline" button. It counts the destination INBOX, not the spelling:
# +tag suffixes are stripped, quoted local parts ("victim"@...) are unquoted, the domain is
# normalised to its ASCII/punycode form, and Gmail's dots and googlemail.com alias are
# folded, so none of victim+1@, "victim"@ or v.i.c.t.i.m@googlemail.com buys a fresh
# cooldown. Only published facts about delivery and standard normalisations are folded --
# addresses differing by a Unicode confusable stay DIFFERENT keys, since folding those would
# be a guess about identity and would spend a stranger's allowance. Valid range 1-1440, and
# it must stay inside
# INVITE_EVENT_RETENTION_DAYS below -- startup fails on that pair, because the rows this
# counts are swept on that window.
#INVITE_RECIPIENT_COOLDOWN_MINUTES=60
# INVITATIONS one address may receive per day from senders other than you, plus how many of
# your own you may send to it -- one number, spent on both. Counted as "my own sends one
# each, every OTHER sender once": counting raw sends would let a single account spend a
# named person's whole allowance and stop EVERY workspace on the instance from inviting
# them for the day. This way five throwaway accounts inviting one victim once each still
# trip it at the sixth, while any one account is capped at 5 sends to one inbox and takes
# one of the five slots as far as everybody else is concerned. Everything keyed on the
# sender multiplies by the number of accounts an attacker holds; this one does not. The
# known cost: at the sixth invitation an innocent admin learns the address has been invited
# from workspaces they cannot see -- one bit, only to somebody who already administers
# members and already knows the address, and it is the REFUSAL'S WORDING that keeps it at one
# bit. (Its Retry-After coarsens a DEADLINE rather than a duration, so every probe reports the
# same moment instead of stepping down onto the real one; that raises the price of another
# tenant's send instant rather than hiding it, since a caller who waits for the refusal to
# lift learns it anyway.)
#
# IF IT PROVES NOISY, RAISE IT RATHER THAN REMOVING IT -- BUT IN SMALL STEPS. Because the
# count is "own sends one each, other senders once", N colluding accounts can put
# N x (cap - N + 1) messages into one inbox in a day, i.e. floor((cap+1)^2 / 4) at worst:
# 9/day at 5, but 30/day at 10. The bound is QUADRATIC, so doubling this number triples the
# harassment ceiling it permits.
#INVITE_MAX_PER_RECIPIENT_PER_DAY=5
# Days of mail_send_events history kept. It must outlast EVERY ceiling window, because a
# swept row is a row no ceiling can count -- set it below one and that ceiling silently
# shortens to it, so STARTUP FAILS on the pair instead. THERE ARE TWO WINDOWS: the cooldown
# above (configurable) and the FIXED 24h window of INVITE_MAX_PER_RECIPIENT_PER_DAY, which no
# variable can lower. Startup compares the retention against the WIDER of the two, which is
# why the valid range starts at 2 and not 1: one day would be exactly 24h against a 24h
# window, and a replica whose sweep clock runs a second ahead then deletes a row another
# replica's daily count still needs -- the cap under-counts and permits a send it meant to
# refuse, with no error and no log line. The rest of the window is forensic: after the
# MailDailyVolumeHigh alert fires, this table is the only place that can answer WHO and
# WHICH ADDRESSES -- the metrics cannot, because no metric here is allowed to carry an
# address or an id. Short enough that this is not a durable record of who-emailed-whom.
# The sweep runs whether or not RATE_LIMIT_ENABLED is true.
#INVITE_EVENT_RETENTION_DAYS=7

# ── Custom roles ───────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Custom roles per workspace, counted across BOTH scopes (workspace + project);
# the 8 built-in templates belong to no workspace and never count. Creating past it is a
# 409. A SPRAWL GUARD, NEVER A LICENCE CHECK — identical in dc and cloud, no profile
# override, no tier. The cap is EXACT, not approximate: creation counts under a row lock
# on the workspace, so concurrent creates queue rather than all passing the same check.
# Valid range 1–500 — an out-of-range value fails startup rather than being clamped.
# THERE IS NO "UNLIMITED" VALUE: 0 is out of range and fails startup, so an operator who
# wants no practical cap writes 500, the highest accepted number.
# To keep the default, leave the line COMMENTED OUT (as below) — not blank. "ROLES_MAX_
# CUSTOM_PER_WORKSPACE=" is an empty value, not an absent one, and binding empty to the
# app's primitive int aborts startup; only an absent variable falls back to 50.
# Note that 500 is not free: the roles list derives, for every role, which other roles it
# could assign — that comparison is quadratic in the number of same-scope roles, and every
# workspace member may call it.
#ROLES_MAX_CUSTOM_PER_WORKSPACE=50

# ── Project access for NEW workspaces ──────────────────────────────
# DEFAULT_PROJECT_ACCESS_MODE (app.workspace.default-project-access-mode) — the
# project-access mode a NEWLY CREATED workspace starts in:
#   OPEN   (default) everyone in the workspace can work in every project, through
#          each project's default role. Add someone to a project only to give them
#          a DIFFERENT role. This is the behaviour every existing workspace has.
#   STRICT only people added to a project can change anything in it. Everyone can
#          still SEE every project — STRICT narrows writes, never reads.
# It applies ONLY at creation. It never moves an existing workspace: change one
# from the UI (Workspace settings → General) or PATCH /api/workspaces/{ws}.
# Demo seeding creates its workspace through the same code path, so setting STRICT
# here gives you a strict demo workspace too — deliberately, with no bypass.
# Identical in dc and cloud: access modes are a product feature, not a plan feature.
# An unrecognised value ABORTS STARTUP rather than falling back — there is no third
# mode, and silently guessing at a security default is worse than not booting.
#DEFAULT_PROJECT_ACCESS_MODE=OPEN

# ── Bootstrap admin ────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The admin is seeded ONLY when BOTH email and password are set — a blank email skips
# seeding entirely, a blank password logs a WARN and skips it (see DataSeeder).
#
# The password ships empty, and the value it used to ship is now REFUSED BY THE APPLICATION
# by name: it repeated its own variable name, so an unedited copy of this file created an
# ACTIVE SYSTEM ADMINISTRATOR whose email and password are both printed in a public
# repository. Two strings and you are an administrator — no forging, nothing to guess, and
# the per-account login backoff never engages, because a correct password is not a failed
# attempt. The symptom of leaving it EMPTY is that nobody can log in, which is the loud half
# of the same problem.
#
# If an upgrade stops booting saying this password is "the value this project published",
# note what it also says: REMOVING THE VARIABLE DOES NOT CHANGE THE ACCOUNT. Seeding is
# idempotent, so the user already exists and keeps that password. Reset it (or delete the
# row) first, and only then set a password of your own here — or none.
#
# AND THE PASSWORD YOU PICK MUST NOT CONTAIN A BARE `$` — see the dollar-sign note at the
# top of this file. Compose expands it, so a generated password gets silently truncated at
# the `$` and the account is seeded with a value you never chose and cannot read back. This
# is the variable where that lands hardest, because nothing later says anything about it.
#
# AND IT HAS A LENGTH CEILING: 72 UTF-8 BYTES. That is BCrypt's own limit, not a policy this
# project chose, so it applies to every password the application stores — the same ceiling
# meets anyone who registers or resets a password. Count BYTES, not characters: plain Latin
# letters cost 1 each, accented, Greek and Cyrillic 2, most other scripts 3, emoji 4 — so a
# 40-character Cyrillic passphrase is already 80 bytes and over the limit, while the 64 ASCII
# characters of `openssl rand -base64 48` are inside it and stronger than BCrypt can use.
# Over the limit the application REFUSES TO START — it does not truncate and does not warn —
# with a message naming this variable and the byte count you actually set. It refuses only
# when the value would be used to CREATE the administrator account: SEED_ADMIN_EMAIL set, the
# value over 72 bytes, and no account occupying that address yet. An installation that has
# already seeded its administrator goes on booting whatever you put here, because seeding is
# idempotent and never re-passwords an existing user.
#
# The address below is a RESERVED example domain on purpose (RFC 2606). A live one is a
# trap: fill in the password, forget the email, and you have seeded a real administrator at
# an address you do not own — and "forgot password" is public, so a reset token for your
# instance goes to somebody else's mail server.
SEED_ADMIN_EMAIL=admin@your-domain.example
SEED_ADMIN_PASSWORD=
# Optional — defaults to "Admin".
#SEED_ADMIN_DISPLAY_NAME=Admin

# ── Public URL ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# APP_BASE_URL — used for email links, robots.txt/sitemap.xml and OG meta
# tags. MUST be the real public URL in prod (defaults to http://localhost:8080).
APP_BASE_URL=https://your-domain.example
# SITE_ADDRESS — the address(es) Caddy serves and gets Let's Encrypt certs for.
# Its host MUST match APP_BASE_URL's host. Comma-separate apex + www — e.g.
# `example.com, www.example.com`. Empty because ${SITE_ADDRESS:?...} on the caddy service
# fires on absence only: a placeholder domain interpolates, Caddy accepts it, and the
# failure moves to an ACME challenge for a domain you do not own.
SITE_ADDRESS=

# ── Email (SMTP) ───────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Defaults point at the dev MailHog (localhost:1025). In prod set a real
# SMTP server, otherwise email verification (which doubles as login) fails.
MAIL_HOST=
MAIL_PORT=587
MAIL_USERNAME=
MAIL_PASSWORD=
MAIL_SMTP_AUTH=true
MAIL_STARTTLS=true
MAIL_FROM=noreply@your-domain.example
# SMTP socket timeouts (ms) — a black-holed mail host fails a worker fast instead
# of hanging. Defaults are generous for real SMTP under normal load.
#MAIL_SMTP_CONNECT_TIMEOUT_MS=5000    # TCP connect timeout
#MAIL_SMTP_READ_TIMEOUT_MS=10000      # per-read (server response) timeout
#MAIL_SMTP_WRITE_TIMEOUT_MS=10000     # per-write (send) timeout

# ── Mail delivery reliability ──────────────────────────────────────
# Bounded mail executor — mail can't starve other async work or spawn a
# thread-per-task under an SMTP stall. A full queue DROPS the message and writes a
# failed_email row; it never sends on the request thread, so a slow mail host cannot
# take Tomcat workers with it.
# Valid ranges 1-50 / 1-50 / 1-10000. EVERY MAIL_* AND APP_STOP_GRACE_SECONDS VALUE IN
# THIS SECTION BINDS AS AN int: a blank line (MAIL_ASYNC_MAX_POOL=) is not "unset", it
# stops the boot. Comment the line out to take the default.
#MAIL_ASYNC_CORE_POOL=2               # steady-state mail-sender threads
#MAIL_ASYNC_MAX_POOL=5                # max mail-sender threads under load
#MAIL_ASYNC_QUEUE_CAPACITY=100        # queued mails before new ones are dead-lettered
# Shutdown: wait this long for queued mail to flush, THEN dead-letter whatever is
# left. Read it together with the queue capacity above and with APP_STOP_GRACE_SECONDS
# (further up this file) — all three steps have to finish inside the container's stop
# grace, and the app REFUSES TO START if they do not, naming all three. So RAISING THIS
# ALONE IS REFUSED: raise APP_STOP_GRACE_SECONDS in the same edit, which moves both the
# container's stop_grace_period and the app's copy of it. At the default 30s grace the
# practical ceiling here is about 28. Valid range 1-120.
#MAIL_ASYNC_SHUTDOWN_DRAIN_SECONDS=15 # graceful drain before dead-lettering the rest
# What the drain CANNOT cover: messages a worker has already started sending are
# interrupted, get no failed_email row, and are named only as an upper bound in the
# shutdown WARN. Up to MAIL_ASYNC_MAX_POOL of them per deploy.
# CRITICAL mail (verification + password reset) retries with backoff, then
# dead-letters to failed_email; invite mail stays best-effort (no retry).
#MAIL_CRITICAL_MAX_ATTEMPTS=3         # send attempts before dead-lettering
#MAIL_CRITICAL_RETRY_BACKOFF_MS=2000  # base backoff between retries (ms)
# WHAT KEEPS failed_email BOUNDED. Until 0.18.0 nothing did, and nothing had to: a row
# cost one exhausted retry cycle, so the mail pool was the throttle. A row now also
# records a message that was NEVER ATTEMPTED (full queue / shutdown), and that costs one
# refused dispatch — so the write rate became the request rate, which on the
# unauthenticated paths is bounded per IP and not globally. Two bounds, because either
# alone leaves a hole: retention bounds the table in TIME, the cap bounds it in RATE.
# Retention is long on purpose — these rows are the only record that somebody's password
# reset never went, and they are what a re-drive reads. Valid range 7-3650.
#MAIL_FAILED_EMAIL_RETENTION_DAYS=90  # daily sweep deletes dead-letter rows older than this
# Per instance, per hour, never-attempted rows only. Valid range 1-1000000. Above it the
# loss is still logged at ERROR (type + recipient DOMAIN, never the address) and still
# reaches the caller's error line — it just stops being durable — and a line naming the
# suppressed count is logged when the hour rolls. IF YOU ARE HITTING THIS, THE MAIL POOL IS
# SATURATED: look at SMTP and at MAIL_ASYNC_* before raising it.
# NODE-LOCAL, and the window is a fixed hour that starts at the first write: N replicas
# write up to N x this into ONE table, and a restart re-arms the window — so a rolling
# deploy, which is the event most likely to produce these rows, also resets the counter
# that bounds them. Size the TABLE against cap x replicas. Raising it by an order of
# magnitude outgrows the retention sweep, which is an un-indexed DELETE; and the capped
# never-attempted rows are only one of the table's two writers — a fast-failing SMTP host
# (connection refused, NXDOMAIN) drives the UNCAPPED tried-and-failed half far harder.
#MAIL_NEVER_ATTEMPTED_MAX_PER_HOUR=500

# ── Attachment storage ─────────────────────────────────────────────
# The default follows SPRING_PROFILES_ACTIVE: cloud -> s3, dc -> local
# (on-disk attachments_data volume). Uncomment STORAGE_TYPE only to
# override that per-model default (e.g. run Cloud on local disk).
#STORAGE_TYPE=s3
# Required only when the effective storage type is s3:
STORAGE_S3_BUCKET=
STORAGE_S3_REGION=
# S3 client timeouts (ms). Bound a hung S3 to <= api-call-ms total; read-ms is the
# ceiling per socket read (a large attachment streams over one socket). Only take
# effect when the effective storage type is s3; local FS ignores them.
#STORAGE_S3_CONNECT_TIMEOUT_MS=3000            # TCP connect timeout
#STORAGE_S3_READ_TIMEOUT_MS=20000              # per socket-read timeout
#STORAGE_S3_API_CALL_TIMEOUT_MS=30000          # total per-request budget (incl. retries)
#STORAGE_S3_API_CALL_ATTEMPT_TIMEOUT_MS=10000  # budget per single attempt

# ── Logging ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Log levels. In the cloud/dc profiles the app emits one JSON object per
# line to stdout (for the observability pipeline); local/default stay readable.
LOG_LEVEL=INFO
LOG_LEVEL_APP=INFO

# ── Metrics / Actuator ─────────────────────────────────────────────
# The app exposes Prometheus metrics + health on a SEPARATE management port.
# Keep it INTERNAL — never add a compose `ports:` mapping and never proxy it
# through Caddy. Only in-network Prometheus should reach it.
MANAGEMENT_PORT=9090
MANAGEMENT_ENDPOINTS=health,prometheus,info
METRICS_EXPORT_ENABLED=true

# ── Observability stack (OPTIONAL) ─────────────────────────────────
# Only needed if you run the extra observability compose file:
#   docker compose -f docker-compose.prod.yml -f docker-compose.observability.yml up -d
# It adds Loki (logs) + Alloy (collector) + Grafana (dashboards). Nothing is
# exposed publicly; Grafana binds 127.0.0.1:3000 — reach it via an SSM
# port-forward (see docs/ops-prod-hardening.md §4).
GF_SECURITY_ADMIN_USER=admin
# Required when the observability stack runs: ${GF_SECURITY_ADMIN_PASSWORD:?...} aborts
# the whole `up` while it is empty. That is why it ships empty rather than carrying a
# strong-sounding placeholder, which would have interpolated straight into Grafana's admin
# login and locked nobody out.
#   openssl rand -base64 24
GF_SECURITY_ADMIN_PASSWORD=
# How long Loki keeps logs (default 7 days):
LOKI_RETENTION_PERIOD=168h
# Prometheus metrics retention (whichever limit is hit first):
PROMETHEUS_RETENTION_TIME=15d
PROMETHEUS_RETENTION_SIZE=2GB
# postgres-exporter DB login. Least privilege: create a read-only pg_monitor
# role and set these (see the "Observability" section of docs/self-hosting.md for
# the CREATE ROLE SQL). If left unset, it falls back to the app DB_USERNAME/
# DB_PASSWORD so metrics still work out of the box.
#DB_MONITOR_USER=hamstrack_exporter
#DB_MONITOR_PASSWORD=
# Alerting: where Grafana emails alerts (AppDown, Postgres down, high error rate,
# disk filling, email failures, JVM heap, stale backups…). Grafana's SMTP reuses
# your MAIL_* settings above.
# Required when the observability stack runs (fail-fast otherwise): an empty value
# makes Grafana reject the alerting provisioning entirely, so the RULES die with the
# contact point, not just the delivery — and Grafana then refuses to start and
# crash-loops, while `docker compose up -d` still exits 0. The deploy reports success
# and only `docker compose ps` shows the dead container. Use an address (or a group
# alias) that a human reads on a weekend.
#
# Shipped EMPTY on purpose, as every guarded value in this file now is. The guard
# in the compose file is ${OBS_ALERT_EMAIL_TO:?...}, which fires on unset or empty
# and NOT on "non-empty and wrong" — a placeholder here would interpolate cleanly
# and hand Grafana a contact point addressed to the placeholder, which is the exact
# defect the guard exists to remove. A wrong admin password announces itself at the
# login screen; a wrong alert destination announces nothing at all.
#
# What the refusal costs: both compose files are layered in ONE command, so an
# unset/empty value aborts the whole invocation — your app's `up -d`, and equally
# `down`, `stop`, `ps` and `logs` when passed with both -f files. Interpolation is
# resolved before anything is created, changed or stopped, so nothing is modified
# and a running stack keeps running; you simply cannot drive it until this is set.
#
# Dashboards but no alert mail (a self-hoster with no SMTP at all)? Set this to any
# address you own AND set GF_SMTP_ENABLED=false below: the rules then provision,
# evaluate, and show their state in Grafana -> Alerting, with nothing delivered.
# The address itself is not skippable — the contact point must have one.
OBS_ALERT_EMAIL_TO=
# Grafana SMTP on/off. Default true - alerts go out over the MAIL_* settings above.
# Set false for "evaluate, do not deliver" (see the note above). Grafana's sender
# address is MAIL_FROM, shared with the app.
#GF_SMTP_ENABLED=true
# External URL Grafana uses to build links (in alert emails, among others). The
# default matches the port-forward that every runbook here assumes; change it only
# if you front Grafana with a proxy on a real hostname.
#GF_SERVER_ROOT_URL=http://localhost:3000
